  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\n(Friday - Friday Night)\n\nThe position of the quasi-stationary front Friday morning is \nuncertain, but should tend to push northward during the day. \nConvergence near this feature though looks very modest, so it is \nuncertain if it will be strong enough to help promote any convective \ninitiation during peak heating. There is an even weaker low-level \njet forecast for Friday night (~15-20 knots), but weak moisture \nconvergence may interact with the surface boundary to yield at least \nscattered showers and thunderstorms along/poleward of the boundary.\n\n\n(Saturday - Monday Night)\n\nDeterministic models are in good agreement that the front will shift \nnorth of the area as a warm front by Saturday. This should leave at \nleast most of our area dry, but with increasing heat and humidity \nbeginning this weekend. Peak heat index values Saturday afternoon \nmay crest the century mark roughly along/south of I-70, and \npotentially over the entire area Sunday and Monday. Magnitude heat \nadvisory criteria (105+) is also possible, especially Sunday and \nMonday. There is a fly in the ointment for Sunday (and maybe even \nMonday), at least in our northern counties. With the heart of the \nmid/upper level anticyclone centered well to our west, we may be \nsusceptible to seeing the southern end of an MCS either or both \nSunday/Monday mornings. If this occurs, that could provide enough \nclouds and/or an outflow boundary, which could limit the heat \nthreat. \n\n\n(Tuesday - Wednesday)\n\nThere is still plenty of spread with the strength of a mid/upper \nlevel trough crossing the Great Lakes/southeast Canada early next \nweek. Clusters of the 500-hPa height pattern show this quite well, \nwith roughly half of LREF members showing a stronger trough whereas \nthe other half is weaker. The stronger trough solution would be much \nmore likely to send some type of backdoor cold front through the \nregion and help end the hot/humid pattern. The weaker trough \nscenario likely would keep the same air mass in place, which likely \nwould mean a more prolonged period of heat/humidity.",
